	<p>Tanzania is one of Africa’s most culturally diverse nations, home to more than 
	120 distinct ethnic groups, each with its own language, traditions, and artistic 
	expressions. Although most Tanzanian languages share Bantu roots, they differ 
	significantly in vocabulary, pronunciation, and grammar, creating a rich linguistic 
	landscape united by the national language, Kiswahili.</p>
	
	<h2>Ethnic and Linguistic Diversity</h2>
	
	<p>The country’s ethnic groups fall into three broad families: Bantu-speaking 
	agriculturalists (about 95% of the population), Nilotic pastoralists such as the Maasai, 
	and indigenous hunter-gatherers like the Hadzabe. Major groups include the Sukuma, 
	Chagga, Nyamwezi, Haya, Maasai, Makonde, and Zaramo, none of which forms a majority. 
	This absence of a dominant tribe has fostered a culture of coexistence and mutual 
	respect, with Swahili serving as a unifying linguistic bridge across communities.</p>
	
	<h2>Religious Pluralism</h2>
	
	<p>Tanzania’s religious landscape mirrors its ethnic diversity. Christianity and 
	Islam are the two largest faiths, with roughly 57% Christian and 31% Muslim, 
	alongside smaller communities practicing traditional African religions, Hinduism, 
	and Buddhism. The mainland has large Roman Catholic, Lutheran, Anglican, and 
	Pentecostal communities, while Islam predominates along the coast and in Zanzibar, 
	where Islamic learning dates back over a thousand years. The Indian diaspora 
	maintains vibrant Hindu and Buddhist temples in urban areas, adding to the country’s 
	spiritual mosaic.</p>
	
	<h2>Culinary Traditions</h2>
	
	<p>Tanzanian cuisine reflects centuries of trade and migration. Coastal dishes feature 
	coconut milk, spices, rice, and seafood, with Zanzibar’s pilau and biryani showcasing 
	Indian Ocean influences. Inland communities enjoy staples like ugali (maize meal), 
	nyama choma (grilled meat), ndizi (plantains), maharage (beans), and chapati. 
	Street food such as samosas and mishkaki (skewered meat) is integral to daily life, 
	and sharing meals remains a core social tradition.</p>
	
	<h2>Festivals and Celebrations</h2>
	
	<p>Annual festivals like Saba Saba (Industry Day) and the Nguvumali Festival highlight 
	regional music, dance, and storytelling, reinforcing cultural pride and intercommunity 
	bonds. Zanzibar’s Sauti za Busara music festival and the Mwaka Kogwa new-year rites at 
	Makunduchi further illustrate the country’s vibrant ceremonial life.</p>
	<p>Tanzania’s cultural diversity expressed through language, religion, art, music, and 
	cuisine—forms the essence of its national identity, making it a living tapestry of 
	African heritage and global influences.</p>
	
	<h2>Art, Music, and Dance</h2>
	
	<p>Each ethnic group contributes unique artistic traditions. The Maasai are renowned 
	for intricate beadwork and warrior ceremonies, while the Makonde are celebrated for 
	expressive wood carvings. Traditional ngoma dances are performed at weddings, harvests, 
	and initiation rites across the country. On the coast, taarab music blends African 
	rhythms with Arab melodies, creating a sound unique to East Africa. Modern Tanzania 
	has gained international recognition for Bongo Flava, a genre mixing hip-hop, 
	Afrobeat, and Swahili lyrics.</p>
	
